## General

- Be concise and precise. Assume the user is an experienced programmer.
- Treat questions as questions: re-evaluate when challenged, and do not implement a queried alternative unless requested.
- Keep changes and external actions within the requested task’s scope; preserve unrelated work.
- Prefer `rg` for text search and `jq` for JSON manipulation.

## Version control

- Use `jj` for every version-control operation. Never run the `git` executable; commands under `jj git` are allowed.
- If an operation appears to require the `git` executable, stop and ask instead.
- Split work into atomic, bisectable commits: one coherent change per commit, including its tests, with each commit buildable and passing relevant checks. Keep unrelated refactors and formatting separate.
- Always leave an empty working-copy revision (`@`) on top of named (described) changes when handing work back.

{{- if .work_laptop }}

## Lunar repositories

- In repositories under `~/code/lunar`, run tests directly rather than through `shuttle run test`.
- If tests need local infrastructure, use `shuttle run local_up`. If they need environment variables, use `shuttle run generate_dotenv`.
- When investigating another Lunar repository, prefer an existing local clone and update it with `jj`; otherwise clone it or use the organization Sourcegraph instance via `src`.
{{- end }}

## Code changes

- Verify code changes with the relevant project checks. If a relevant check wasn’t run, say why.
- Put resumable notes in `tmp/notes/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>.md`.
- Never commit production data. Anonymize any PII retained in tests, comments, or commit descriptions.

## GitHub

- Prefer `gh` over fetching GitHub web pages directly. When already in the relevant repository, prefer local history over the GitHub API.
